        <mxCell id="header-sub" value="&lt;span style=&quot;font-variant-ligatures:none&quot;&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#1e293b&quot; style=&quot;font-size:13px&quot;&gt;What happens, in order, when an operator runs cnp.sh up for a public cloud?&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;Scope: EKS and GKE alike, written &amp;lt;cloud&amp;gt; · main as of 2026-10-29 · derived from code: Cloud/terraform/public/{aws/EKS,gcp/GKE}/{cnp.sh,main.tf}, modules/cluster-addons, platform/kustomization.yaml, K3s/k8s/app/{platform-clouds,wireguard-clouds}.yaml, K3s/k8s/config/wireguard/settings*.env, CMP/backend/app/services/clusters/state.py&lt;br&gt;Prerequisites: ssh pae-node-1 open with DynamicForward 1080 · VAULT_ADDR=https://vault.3istor.com and vault login · AWS_PROFILE=cnp · gcloud ADC (GKE) · terraform.tfvars. Once by hand: vault kv put kvv2/cnp-vpn/&amp;lt;cloud&amp;gt; and the mount kubernetes-&amp;lt;cloud&amp;gt;. What down removes: docs/06-multicloud/07-procedures-operateur.md&lt;br&gt;Arrows: purple = background request, red = authentication, amber = tunnel, grey = traffic. Notes in grey say what survives cnp.sh down and the known gaps.&lt;br&gt;v0.3 · source: cnp-docs/architecture/cnp&lt;/span&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontFamily=JetBrains Mono,Noto Sans Mono,DejaVu Sans Mono,monospace;fontSize=11;fontColor=#475569;" vertex="1" parent="1">
